#metadata("Walking Trees Without Parents") <title>

= Walking Trees Without Parents

Most tree APIs hand you a node and leave you to find your way back up. That is
fine while you are only reading _leaves_, and it stops being fine the moment a
consumer needs to know *where* it is: which section a paragraph belongs to,
whether the thing it is looking at sits inside a footnote, how deep the nesting
around it goes. See @navigation for what that costs.

The usual answer is a parent pointer on every node. It is also the answer that
makes every node bigger, every construction step slower, and every tree harder
to share between documents.#footnote[A shared subtree cannot carry a parent
pointer at all: it has more than one parent, and neither of them is wrong.]

== What a walk already knows

A depth-first walk knows the whole chain, for free, at every moment: it is
sitting on the Go stack, one frame per open element. The only problem is that
nothing hands it to the caller.

- The chain is exact — it is the recursion itself.
- It costs nothing to maintain beyond what the recursion already pays.
- It is valid only while the walk is at that node, which is both the catch and
  the reason it is cheap.

That last point is the whole design. A cursor that is valid for the length of
one loop body needs no invalidation story, no cache, and no immutability
guarantee from the tree it walks over.

== Filtering earns its keep

#link("https://pkg.go.dev/golang.org/x/tools/go/ast/inspector")[The inspector
package] makes the same observation about Go's syntax trees, and adds a second:
a caller almost never wants every node. It wants headings, or metadata, or the
one element type it knows how to render.

+ Say which kinds you want.
+ The walk hands you those.
+ Everything else is walked through, not handed over.

#table(
    columns: 3,
    table.header[Consumer][Wants][Today],
    [outline], [headings], [type switch per node],
    [footnotes], [footnotes], [type switch per node],
