=== The pruning case

Counting a subtree's nodes so you can skip them one by one is the shape a walk
takes when it cannot decline to descend. It is $O(n)$ work to avoid $O(n)$ work,
and it reads exactly as badly as it sounds:

```go
case *value.Footnote:
    for range value.All(c.Body) {
        skip++
    }
```

With pruning it is one line, and the subtree is never touched:

```go
case *value.Footnote:
    c.SkipChildren()
```

== Numbers, eventually <navigation>

An index over a flat array of push/pop events buys one more thing: skipping a
subtree that provably contains nothing you asked for, in constant time. Whether
that is worth building depends on the shape of real documents, which is a
question only a benchmark answers.

The relationship between what a walk touches and what it yields is roughly
$ t = sum_(i=1)^n c_i quad "versus" quad y = sum_(i in K) c_i $
where $K$ is the set of kinds the caller asked for. When $K$ is small and the
document is large, the gap is the whole argument for an index.#footnote[And when
$K$ is everything, an index is pure overhead — it is a second traversal to
build, and the first one already gave the answer.]

== What this leaves out

/ Random access: A walk-scoped cursor cannot answer questions about siblings it
  has not reached yet.
/ Outliving the walk: The chain unwinds; a caller that needs to keep it takes a
  copy.
/ Positions: Realized content carries no spans, so there is no analog of
  finding the innermost node at a byte offset.

None of those are needed by anything in this repository today, which is the
honest reason they are not here.
